# Benchmark inputs: large, regenerable, not pushed.
# The .pt dumps are the source of truth and are kept in-tree but untracked;
# bins/ and sparse_bins/ are derived from them by the *_pt_to_bin.py scripts.
*.pt
bins/
sparse_bins/
prefill_bins/

# Compiled benchmarks
bench_sparse_amx
bench_sparse_onednn
bench_dense_amx
bench_dense_onednn
prefill_bins_*
prefill_sparse_bins/
libflashmla_*.so
amx_prefill_*.so

# Dense-causal prefill: reference/baseline only, not in the live path. They are
# per-head MHA, so they need per-head K/V (10.7 GB/layer at 128k) and are
# O(Lin^2) -- unusable at long context. They produced the oneDNN-vs-AMX
# crossover at Sq~640 and a golden to check the sparse kernel against; that
# knowledge is recorded, the code is kept on disk but not tracked.
bench_prefill_dense_amx.cpp
bench_prefill_dense_onednn.cpp
prefill_gen_bins.py
prefill_dispatch.h
